App 26: The Replication Tracker
Completeness status: COMPLETE. All sections delivered in one pass. No truncation. Formatted for copy-paste. Long lists are broken into short lines.

1. What It Is
A provenance-aware, versioned database of replication attempts. A replication attempt is any effort to independently repeat a published result, whether it succeeded, failed, or produced a partial or inconclusive outcome.

The Replication Tracker records each attempt with:

the original claim

the replication method

the outcome

the effect sizes

the reasons for any difference

the provenance

It reuses:

the Physics Knowledge Library (App 23)

the Full-Pipeline Simulator (App 9)

the Null-Model Protocol Library (App 18)

the Provenance-Aware Annotation Database (App 20)

the Numerical Verification Tool (App 22)

It is for researchers, meta-scientists, journal editors, funders, and anyone who wants to know which results have been independently tested.

2. Core Requirements
Functional requirements:

Accept deposits of replication attempts from users.

Store each replication attempt with its original claim, its method, its outcome, and its provenance.

Link each replication attempt to the original claim in the Physics Knowledge Library.

Record the original paper reference and the replication paper reference.

Record the replication method, including the sample size, the instruments, and the statistical pipeline.

Record the replication outcome: success, failure, partial, inconclusive.

Record the effect size in the original study and the effect size in the replication.

Record the difference between the original and the replication, and the reason for the difference.

Record the replication date, the replicator, and the replicator's affiliation.

Enforce provenance completeness. Every deposit has a depositor, a date, a source, and a tool version.

Enforce versioning. Every deposit has a version, and updates create new versions.

Enforce supersession acyclicity.

Support querying by replication ID, original claim ID, field, subfield, outcome, replicator, replication date, and keywords.

Support full-text search of the replication description, the method, and the notes.

Support aggregation queries. Count by field, by outcome, by year, by replicator, by effect-size change.

Support exporting a replication attempt as JSON, PROV-O, and Turtle.

Support importing a replication attempt from JSON.

Support bulk import and bulk export.

Support a REST API for CRUD operations.

Support a GraphQL API for flexible queries.

Support a command-line interface for administration.

Support authentication and authorization.

Support audit logging.

Support backup and restore.

Support a web interface for depositing, browsing, and querying.

Support moderation. Replication attempts can be marked as pending, approved, or rejected.

Support linking a replication attempt to a STAG annotation if the original claim has one.

Support linking a replication attempt to a Null-Model Protocol result if the replication used one.

Support linking a replication attempt to a numerical verification result.

Support the replication map. A visualisation of replication attempts by field, by outcome, and by effect-size change.

Support the replication funnel. A visualisation of the path from the original study to the replication.

Non-functional requirements:

Deterministic. Same inputs produce same outputs.

Fast. Query a typical replication attempt in under 100 milliseconds. Aggregate over 10,000 attempts in under one second.

Scalable. Support up to 10^6 replication attempts.

Versioned. The tracker records the STAG schema version, the Null-Model Protocol schema version, the replication schema version, and the tracker version.

Reproducible. The tracker records the provenance for every deposit.

Portable. Works on Linux, macOS, and Windows.

Well-documented. API documentation, replication schema documentation, and user guide.

